### Slice 2: Reusable Components (Building your own Lego blocks)

Right now, if we wanted to put 10 brutalist buttons on our website, we would have to copy and paste that `<button>` HTML and all of its CSS 10 times. If we later decided to change the border thickness from `3px` to `4px`, we would have to find and change it in 10 different places. That is a nightmare!

**Vue solves this using Components.**
A Component is simply us taking that button, putting it in its own file, and turning it into our very own custom HTML tag. Let's do it.

---

### Step 1: Create the Component File
1. In your project, go into the `src/Components/` folder.
2. Create a new folder inside it called `ui`.
3. Inside that `ui` folder, create a new file named `BrutalistButton.vue`.

Open `BrutalistButton.vue` and paste this code:

```vue
<template>
  <button class="brutalist-button">
    <!-- The <slot></slot> tag is a special Vue placeholder. 
         Whatever text we type inside our custom tag later will magically appear right here! -->
    <slot></slot>
  </button>
</template>

<style scoped>
/* Notice the word "scoped" above? It means these styles will ONLY ever apply to this specific button. */
.brutalist-button {
  background-color: #ffd60a;
  border: 3px solid #000000;
  font-size: 1rem;
  font-weight: 800;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  padding: 12px 24px;
  cursor: pointer;
  box-shadow: 4px 4px 0px 0px #000000;
  transition: transform 0.1s ease, box-shadow 0.1s ease;
}

.brutalist-button:hover {
  transform: translate(2px, 2px);
  box-shadow: 2px 2px 0px 0px #000000;
}

.brutalist-button:active {
  transform: translate(4px, 4px);
  box-shadow: 0px 0px 0px 0px #000000;
}
</style>
```

---

### Step 2: Use your new Lego block in `App.vue`
Now, go back to your `src/App.vue` file. We are going to:
1. Import our new custom block at the top.
2. Delete the old, ugly normal `<button>` and use our shiny new `<BrutalistButton>`.
3. Delete the button CSS from `App.vue` (since it now lives inside the button's own file).

Change your `App.vue` to look like this:

```vue
<script setup>
// 1. We import our custom Lego block here!
import BrutalistButton from './Components/ui/BrutalistButton.vue'
</script>

<template>
  <div class="preview-container">

    <div class="brutalist-card">
      <h1 class="card-title">Macondo Reader</h1>
      <p class="card-description">
        Look at this! We are now using a reusable component. We can easily spawn multiple buttons now.
      </p>

      <!-- 2. We use our custom component just like a regular HTML tag! -->
      <!-- The text "Start Reading" falls directly into the <slot></slot> placeholder. -->
      <BrutalistButton>Start Reading</BrutalistButton>

      <br><br> <!-- Adding some line breaks for spacing -->

      <BrutalistButton>Add to Library</BrutalistButton>
    </div>

  </div>
</template>

<style>
/* 3. The card and container CSS stays the same. 
      But ALL the .brutalist-button CSS has been completely deleted from here! */

.preview-container {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
  min-height: 100vh;
  background-color: #faf6ee; 
  font-family: sans-serif;
  margin: 0;
}

.brutalist-card {
  background-color: #ffffff;
  border: 4px solid #000000;
  padding: 24px;
  max-width: 400px;
  box-shadow: 8px 8px 0px 0px #000000; 
}

.card-title {
  margin-top: 0;
  font-size: 2rem;
  font-weight: 900;
  text-transform: uppercase;
}

.card-description {
  color: #333333;
  margin-bottom: 24px;
  line-height: 1.5;
}
</style>
```

