Your `App.vue` is nice and clean. Here's the gameplan — we'll do this in two parts:

### Part 1: Sidebar Toggles

To hide/show the sidebars, we need to introduce a tiny bit of JavaScript logic directly in `App.vue`. This is the first time we're using actual JavaScript variables in our project, so here's how it works:

In Vue, when you create a variable using `ref()`, Vue watches it like a hawk. The moment the variable changes, Vue instantly re-draws the screen. We'll create two variables: `showSidebar` and `showRightPanel`, and use a Vue command called `v-if` to show/hide each panel.

Update your `App.vue` to this:

```vue
<style>
@import 'tailwindcss';
</style>

<script setup>
import { ref } from 'vue'
import rightpanel from './Components/layout/rightpanel.vue'
import sidebar from './Components/layout/sidebar.vue'

// Two light switches! true = ON (visible), false = OFF (hidden)
const showSidebar = ref(true)
const showRightPanel = ref(true)
</script>

<template>
  <div class="app-container">

    <!-- v-if means: "Only show this component if the variable is true" -->
    <sidebar v-if="showSidebar"></sidebar>

    <main class="main-content">

      <!-- The Top Bar -->
      <div class="top-bar">
        <!-- These buttons flip the light switches! -->
        <button class="toggle-btn" @click="showSidebar = !showSidebar">☰ Menu</button>
        <h1 class="top-bar-title">Chaptr</h1>
        <button class="toggle-btn" @click="showRightPanel = !showRightPanel">📖 Details</button>
      </div>

      <div class="content-area">
        <router-view></router-view>
      </div>

    </main>

    <rightpanel v-if="showRightPanel"></rightpanel>

  </div>
</template>

<style scoped>
.app-container {
  display: flex;
  height: 100vh;
  background-color: #faf6ee;
}

.main-content {
  flex-grow: 1;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column; /* Stack the top bar ABOVE the content vertically! */
  overflow-y: auto;
}

/* The Top Bar */
.top-bar {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between; /* Pushes the left button, title, and right button to the edges */
  align-items: center;
  padding: 16px 32px;
  border-bottom: 4px solid #000000;
  background-color: #ffffff;
}

.top-bar-title {
  font-size: 1.5rem;
  font-weight: 900;
  text-transform: uppercase;
}

.toggle-btn {
  background-color: #ffd60a;
  border: 3px solid #000000;
  padding: 8px 16px;
  font-weight: 900;
  font-size: 0.9rem;
  cursor: pointer;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  box-shadow: 3px 3px 0px 0px #000000;
  transition: transform 0.1s ease, box-shadow 0.1s ease;
}

.toggle-btn:hover {
  transform: translate(1px, 1px);
  box-shadow: 2px 2px 0px 0px #000000;
}

.toggle-btn:active {
  transform: translate(3px, 3px);
  box-shadow: 0px 0px 0px 0px #000000;
}

/* The area below the top bar where the router content lives */
.content-area {
  padding: 40px;
  flex-grow: 1;
}
</style>
```

### What is happening here:

1. **`const showSidebar = ref(true)`** — This creates a light switch that starts in the ON position.
2. **`v-if="showSidebar"`** — This tells Vue: *"Only render the sidebar if the light switch is ON."*
3. **`@click="showSidebar = !showSidebar"`** — The `!` means "flip it." If it was `true`, make it `false`. If it was `false`, make it `true`. So every click toggles the sidebar on/off!
4. **The Top Bar** — I built it directly inside `main-content` using `flex-direction: column` so it stacks: Top Bar on top, Router content below it. The `justify-content: space-between` rule pushes the Menu button to the left, the Title to the center, and the Details button to the right.

Save `App.vue`, and you should see a top bar with two clickable buttons that instantly hide/show each sidebar! Try it out and let me know how it feels.

